# (prefix, level, question, tests, approach, answer, mistakes, sixty)
D1 = [
# ---------------- AI PRODUCT IMPROVEMENT ----------------
("AIPI","Hard","ChatGPT image generation has 175M WAU. How would you get it to 350M in 3 months with 3 engineers?",
 "Growth thinking under a tight resource limit.","Find the biggest lever: new-user reach, repeat use, or sharing. Pick two cheap, high-leverage bets.",
 "Assume the base is mostly people who already chat with the product. I would split WAU growth into (1) activation of existing chat users who never tried images, (2) resurrection of lapsed image users, and (3) sharing loops that bring new people. With 3 engineers I would do: an in-chat nudge that offers an image when a conversation naturally calls for one (greetings, birthdays, logos, slides); one-tap templates and style presets that remove the blank-prompt problem; and share links that open a ready-to-edit image with the prompt attached, so every shared picture is an acquisition ad. I would measure image-attempt rate per active user, first-image completion, shares per image and new users from shared links. Cut anything needing new model work. Doubling in three months is aggressive, so I would tell leadership the realistic range and what must be true for the top of it.",
 "Proposing new models or big features that three engineers cannot ship in a quarter.","Existing users, lapsed users, sharing loops; nudge, templates, shareable edit links.") ,
("AIPI","Medium","Claude's code assistant has a 40% thumbs-down rate from non-technical users. You have 2 months and 5 engineers. What would you build?",
 "Segment-specific diagnosis and focus.","Understand why non-technical users are unhappy before building; fix the experience for their job.",
 "I would first read a sample of 100 thumbs-down sessions with reasons. Likely causes: jargon-heavy answers, code they cannot run, missing context, and no way to check the result. Then I would design a mode for non-technical users: plain-language explanations, a 'run it for me' preview, step-by-step setup, safe defaults, and questions asked back when the request is vague. Add a quick reason picker on thumbs-down (too technical, did not work, wrong idea, too slow). Success is thumbs-down rate under 25%, task completion measured by 'did it run', and week-2 return. I would ship an explain-first template in month one and the guided run flow in month two, with evals built from the labelled failures.",
 "Treating all users alike; building before reading the failures.","Diagnose with thumbs-down reasons, build a guided plain-language mode, measure task completion.") ,
("AIPI","Medium","Users spend 30 seconds with ChatGPT's 'thinking' feature, but 60% abandon before seeing results. How would you improve this?",
 "Latency and perceived-wait design.","Reduce real wait, reduce perceived wait, reduce cost of leaving.",
 "Three levers. Real time: route easy questions to a faster path and show 'quick answer now, deeper answer in a moment'. Perceived time: show meaningful progress (what is being checked, partial reasoning summary, a first draft streaming), not a spinner. Cost of waiting: let the user keep chatting, get a notification when the deeper answer is ready, and save the result so leaving does not lose work. Instrument abandonment by second (when do they leave?), by question type, and by device. Target: abandonment under 30% without lowering answer quality. Test with an A/B on progress messaging first because it is cheapest.",
 "Only speeding up the model; ignoring perceived wait.","Faster path, honest progress, background completion with notification; measure abandonment curve.") ,
("AIPI","Medium","Gemini's image generation is 50% slower than competitors. You can't change the model. What do you do?",
 "Product-layer solutions to a technical limit.","Make slowness feel and matter less; match speed to need.",
 "Without touching the model I can still change the experience: stream low-resolution previews early and sharpen, so users see progress within a second; let users start several prompts in parallel; offer a fast draft mode and a quality mode; generate in the background and show results in a gallery; cache and reuse popular prompts; pre-compute style variants for common requests; and warm capacity at peak. I would also improve first-try success with prompt suggestions, since fewer retries reduce total waiting. Metrics: time to first visual, time to accepted image, abandonment during generation, and images per session.",
 "Telling users to wait; promising a model fix outside your control.","Previews, parallel and background generation, draft vs quality modes, fewer retries.") ,
("AIPI","Hard","ChatGPT has a 25% hallucination rate on factual questions. You have 3 months. How do you reduce this without access to the research team?",
 "Product-level reliability levers.","Ground, detect, show, and recover.",
 "At product level: route factual questions to retrieval or browsing with citations; require a source before stating specifics; add a self-check pass that flags unsupported claims and says 'I am not sure' instead; show sources inline and highlight low-confidence statements; offer a 'verify' action; and give users an easy report button feeding an eval set. Build an evaluation set of factual questions by domain, measure hallucination by type, and attack the biggest bucket first (dates, numbers, citations, recent events). Success is a falling rate on the eval set plus rising trust measures, not just fewer complaints. Be careful that abstaining too often hurts helpfulness, so track both.",
 "Promising zero hallucinations; ignoring the helpfulness cost of refusing.","Ground with retrieval, require sources, flag uncertainty, evals by error type.") ,
("AIPI","Medium","Claude's context window is 200K tokens, but only 5% of users use more than 10K. How would you drive adoption of long-context features?",
 "Feature adoption and use-case discovery.","Find jobs that need long context; make it effortless to bring in material.",
 "Users do not know what long context can do. I would identify jobs that benefit: summarising big documents, comparing contracts, analysing a code repository, reviewing meeting archives, research across many PDFs. Then reduce effort: drag-and-drop of folders, connectors to drives and repos, pre-built workflows ('compare these two contracts'), and examples shown in the empty state. Add guidance on cost and speed, and a clear indicator of how much context is used. Measure share of users who upload more than one document, sessions over 10K tokens, retention of those users and task success. Target segments first (legal, finance, research, engineering).",
 "Marketing the number of tokens instead of the jobs it solves.","Jobs-led workflows, easy ingestion, templates, targeted segments.") ,
("AIPI","Hard","Anthropic wants to add image understanding to Claude. You have 6 months and a team of 8. What do you build?",
 "Scoping a new capability.","Pick the top jobs, define MVP, plan phases and risks.",
 "I would start from jobs where images matter: reading screenshots and UI bugs, documents and charts, handwritten notes, product photos, receipts. Month 1 to 2: ship image upload in chat with reliable reading of text, charts and screenshots, plus clear limits (no identifying real people, privacy handling). Month 3 to 4: workflows for the top two jobs (for example chart-to-insight and screenshot-to-bug report) and API access. Month 5 to 6: polish, evals, accessibility, mobile capture, and enterprise controls. Evals cover accuracy on charts and OCR, safety on sensitive images, and latency. Metrics: share of users sending images, task success, repeat use, API adoption. Risks: privacy, misuse, hallucinated details; mitigate with guardrails and uncertainty language.",
 "Building everything at once; ignoring safety and evaluation.","Jobs first, MVP with reading screenshots and charts, workflows next, evals and safety throughout.") ,
("AIPI","Medium","ChatGPT voice mode has 80M MAU but only 20M WAU. How would you increase weekly usage?",
 "Habit formation and use-case fit.","Find the moments voice is better than typing and build triggers for them.",
 "Voice wins when hands or eyes are busy or speaking is faster: driving, cooking, walking, learning languages, brainstorming. A 25% weekly ratio suggests many people tried it but have no habit. I would learn the top use cases from sessions, then build triggers and surfaces: a lock-screen or widget shortcut, car integrations, routines ('daily briefing', 'practice a language for 5 minutes'), smarter wake-up by context, and better interruption handling and speed. Add gentle reminders tied to the user's own use pattern. Metrics: weekly active ratio, sessions per week, session length, and retention of users who adopt a routine.",
 "Generic push notifications; ignoring that voice has specific contexts.","Find contexts, add shortcuts and routines, improve latency, measure weekly ratio.") ,
("AIPI","Medium","Your AI writing assistant has 10M users but only 500K pay for premium. How would you 3x paid conversions in Q4?",
 "Monetisation analysis.","Diagnose the funnel, fix value moments and paywall, test pricing.",
 "Today's rate is 5%. I would find who converts (profession, usage pattern, features) and where free users hit friction. Quick wins: move the paywall to moments of proven value (after a successful rewrite, at a word or credit limit), improve premium previews so users feel the benefit, add annual and student plans, offer team plans for workplaces, and send lifecycle emails to heavy free users. Test price points and trial lengths. Guard against hurting free retention and word of mouth. Target 15% paid uplift per test, tracked by cohort, with revenue and churn as outcome metrics. Three times in a quarter is ambitious, so set milestones and say which levers are most likely.",
 "Cutting the free tier harshly; changing price without testing.","Segment converters, paywall at value moments, plans and trials, test and watch churn.") ,
("AIPI","Medium","Users iterate on AI-generated content 1.2 times on average. How would you increase it to 4+ iterations?",
 "Engagement quality.","Ask whether more iterations is actually good; then improve the refinement loop.",
 "First check that iteration is a good goal: more iterations could mean poor first results. I would define success as final-output acceptance and time to a good result. Then make refinement easy and rewarding: one-tap refinements (shorter, warmer, add example), suggested next edits, side-by-side versions with history, inline editing, and a 'make it more like this' reference. Show what changed. Measure iteration depth alongside acceptance, saved or exported outputs and satisfaction. If acceptance falls while iterations rise, I would rethink the target.",
 "Chasing iteration count without checking quality.","Define good outcome, make refinement one-tap, suggest next edits, track acceptance.") ,
("AIPI","Hard","Your AI code assistant has a 60% activation rate but 70% week-1 churn. You have 8 weeks. What's your plan?",
 "Retention diagnosis.","Understand why activated users leave; fix the first-week value loop.",
 "Activated users tried it but left, so value was not sustained. Weeks 1 to 2: analyse cohorts by what they did and by language and editor, interview churned users, and read sessions around the last use. Likely causes: wrong or slow suggestions, setup friction, trust issues, no new use cases beyond autocomplete. Weeks 3 to 6: ship fixes for the top two causes (for example faster suggestions with better context from the repo, an onboarding checklist that teaches three high-value workflows, and in-editor tips). Weeks 7 to 8: test improvements with a holdout and lifecycle messages for day 2, 4 and 6. Success: week-1 churn under 55% and increase in days used per week.",
 "Adding features before understanding why users left.","Cohort and interview diagnosis; fix quality and onboarding; test with holdout.") ,
("AIPI","Medium","Google Search is adding AI summaries, but users scroll past them. How would you increase engagement?",
 "Product-market fit inside an existing surface.","Understand when summaries are helpful; improve relevance, trust and placement.",
 "Users scroll past when the summary is irrelevant, untrustworthy or slower than the first result. I would segment query types: where summaries help (how-to, comparisons, definitions) and where they do not (navigational, shopping). Show summaries only when confidence and usefulness are high, with visible sources, expandable detail, and follow-up questions. Test placement and length. Metrics: summary engagement, query reformulation rate, successful-session rate, ad and publisher outcomes, and satisfaction. Remember the business tension with link traffic and ads, so balance engagement with ecosystem health.",
 "Forcing summaries on every query; ignoring publishers and ads.","Show when useful and trusted, add sources and follow-ups, measure successful sessions.") ,
("AIPI","Medium","Your AI meeting assistant transcribes perfectly, but users only review 10% of transcripts. How would you increase utility?",
 "Moving from output to outcome.","Users do not want transcripts; they want decisions, actions and answers.",
 "Transcripts are raw data. Utility comes from summaries, action items, decisions and follow-ups delivered where people work. I would generate a short recap with owners and deadlines, push action items to task tools and chat, send a post-meeting email, allow questions across past meetings, and highlight moments relevant to each person. Measure share of meetings where recaps are opened, actions created and completed, search usage and weekly active users. Ask for one-tap corrections to improve trust. Review of transcripts matters less than follow-through.",
 "Improving transcription accuracy when accuracy is already high.","Recap, action items, integrations, ask-across-meetings; measure follow-through.") ,
("AIPI","Hard","Midjourney generates better images than DALL-E, but you have ChatGPT's distribution. How do you compete?",
 "Strategy with distribution advantage.","Compete where distribution beats raw quality; close the gap where it matters.",
 "Distribution means most users meet image generation inside a chat they already use, with no new account. I would compete on workflow rather than pure image quality: conversation-based iteration ('make the sky darker'), editing existing images, brand and character consistency, integration with documents and slides, and speed. Segment users: casual creators care about convenience, professionals care about control and quality. For professionals I would improve quality with style controls and reference images, accepting I may not win them fully. Measure share of image tasks done in-chat, retention versus standalone tools, and conversion of image users to paid. Be honest about what quality gap is acceptable.",
 "Claiming quality parity; ignoring convenience as a real advantage.","Win on convenience and workflow, close quality gap for pros with controls, track in-chat share.") ,
("AIPI","Medium","Your AI tutoring app has great learning outcomes but only 15% retention after 2 weeks. What would you build?",
 "Habit design for education.","Outcomes are good, so the problem is motivation and routine.",
 "Learning is effortful, so I would build motivation and routine: a clear goal and plan, daily short sessions, streaks and progress visuals, reminders at the learner's chosen time, quick wins early, social or parent accountability, and a weekly summary of progress for learners and parents. Make the next step obvious on return. Test goal setting during onboarding and a 5-minute daily mode. Measure day-7 and day-14 retention, sessions per week, and learning outcomes to ensure habit features do not dilute learning.",
 "Adding gamification that distracts from learning.","Goals, short daily sessions, reminders, progress; protect outcomes.") ,
("AIPI","Medium","ChatGPT's mobile app has half the engagement of web. You have 1 designer and 3 engineers. What do you prioritize?",
 "Platform-specific prioritisation.","Find mobile-specific friction and strengths.",
 "Mobile has different jobs: quick questions, voice, photos, on-the-go tasks. Gaps are often keyboard friction, long answers on small screens, slower loads and weaker multi-step work. I would prioritise: faster launch and response, voice and camera as first-class entry points, widgets and shortcuts, concise answer formats and better copy, share and handoff to web for heavy tasks. Instrument session starts, time to first prompt and drop-offs by screen. With a small team, pick two bets for 8 weeks, ship, measure, and iterate.",
 "Copying the web layout to mobile.","Mobile jobs first: speed, voice, camera, shortcuts; measure time to first prompt.") ,
("AIPI","Hard","Enterprise customers want Claude to remember company context across conversations. You have 4 months. How do you build this?",
 "Enterprise memory and trust.","Scope memory, control, security, and phased delivery.",
 "Enterprise memory means company knowledge, not personal chat history. Phase 1 (month 1 to 2): admin-managed knowledge sources (documents, wiki, tickets) with retrieval and citations, per-team spaces and permissions. Phase 2 (month 3): project-level memory of decisions and preferences that users can view, edit and delete. Phase 3 (month 4): audit logs, retention policy, data residency options, and analytics. Principles: least privilege, no cross-customer leakage, no training on customer data, clear visibility of what is remembered. Evals: retrieval accuracy, permission leaks (must be zero), freshness. Metrics: weekly active teams, answers with citations used, security review pass rate.",
 "Treating memory as a feature without permissions and governance.","Knowledge connectors, project memory with controls, audit and retention; zero-leak evals.") ,
("AIPI","Medium","Your AI translation tool is 95% accurate, but users don't trust it for important documents. How do you build trust?",
 "Trust design for AI.","Trust needs evidence, control and recovery, not a better number.",
 "Users judge risk, not average accuracy. I would show what is uncertain (highlight low-confidence phrases), offer glossary and terminology control, side-by-side original and translation with alignment, back-translation checks, and a human review option for critical documents. Provide certification or reviewer marketplace for legal and medical use. Share quality evidence by domain, not a single number. Track document types where users abandon, re-translation requests, review uptake and satisfaction for important documents.",
 "Quoting accuracy; no way to verify or escalate.","Show uncertainty, enable verification, offer human review, publish domain-level evidence.") ,
]
